TCP Split Handshake bypasses stateful firewalls by splitting the 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) across two connections. The attacker sends:
1. **SYN** to server
2. **ACK** to server (before client receives SYN-ACK)
3. Server responds with **RST** when SYN-ACK is received out of order.

To detect this, **Zeek** (formerly Bro) inspects TCP state transitions:
```zeek
event tcp_packet(c: connection, is_orig: bool, flags: tcp_flags, len: count) {
    if ( |flags| == 0x12 && is_orig ) # SYN only
        NOTICE([$note=TCP_Split_Handshake]);
}
```
**nftables** can drop ambiguous handshakes:
```nft
table inet filter {
    chain tcp-check {
        tcp flags & (syn|ack) == syn counter drop
    }
}
```
Real-world evasion seen in **APT34 (OilRig)** campaigns.

